\title{Extremum Seeking Control for Hard-Bound Unstable Systems}

\keywords{Extremum seeking control, nonlinear systems, unstable systems, adaptive control, bounded inputs.}

\begin{abstract}
This paper introduces a constraint-aware extremum seeking control (ESC) framework for a compact buoyancy control device with a linear actuator, designed for underwater robotic applications. The system features inherent instability and strict physical limits on actuator stroke and voltage, posing significant challenges for traditional control methods. The proposed ESC approach optimizes depth regulation performance online while ensuring safe operation within hardware constraints, without requiring an exact plant model. Simulation and experimental results demonstrate that the ESC framework improves input utilization and depth control, maintaining stability and respecting actuator limits in the presence of nonlinearities and uncertainties.
\end{abstract}

\date{Version \versionno, \today}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Oceans cover more than 70\% of the Earth's surface \cite{ocean_ngs,noaa_ocean_exploration} and support climate regulation, marine transportation, offshore energy systems, subsea infrastructure, and scientific monitoring. Even so, persistent underwater operation is still difficult. Missions are expensive, access is limited, and the environment introduces poor visibility, uncertain hydrodynamic disturbances, pressure variation, corrosion, and communication constraints. These practical difficulties continue to motivate autonomous and semi-autonomous underwater robotic systems that can operate with less direct human intervention.

Underwater robotic platforms range from large autonomous underwater vehicles (AUVs) built for long-duration missions to compact bio-inspired robotic fish \cite{masood2025visual} intended for agile motion and operation in tighter spaces. Across that range, depth regulation remains one of the basic control problems. Reliable vertical motion is needed for station keeping, terrain following, inspection, adaptive sampling, and energy-efficient maneuvering. The challenge is that the depth dynamics are influenced by buoyancy, hydrodynamic drag, added mass, pressure-dependent volume changes, actuator limits, and disturbances from the surrounding flow or the vehicle motion itself.

Depth can be regulated through propulsive thrust \cite{tanakitkorn2017depth}, control surfaces \cite{silvestre2007infante}, or body attitude control \cite{hong2010starfish}. These approaches are effective in many vehicles, especially when there is enough forward speed \cite{tanakitkorn2017depth}. For hovering, low-speed operation, or compact bio-inspired platforms \cite{katzschmann2018sofi}, however, continuous thrust-based depth control can consume unnecessary energy and disturb the local flow field \cite{zhao2016hovering}. Variable buoyancy systems (VBSs), also referred to as buoyancy control devices (BCDs), provide another route \cite{tangirala2007vbs}: they change the net buoyant force directly by modifying displaced volume or internal mass distribution \cite{tiwari2020vbs}. That makes them especially attractive for missions where low-speed efficiency matters \cite{carneiro2024depth}.

There is no single preferred VBS architecture. Pump-based systems move fluid between chambers, gas-generation systems create or remove gas volume \cite{koc2025bcd}, thermal or phase-change systems exploit material expansion \cite{hou2020electrothermal}, and mechanical bladder, bellows, or piston-cylinder devices adjust displaced volume using compact actuators. The tradeoff is usually among response speed, energy use, packaging complexity, pressure tolerance, repeatability, and robustness to hydrostatic loading. Those tradeoffs become sharper for small robotic fish and compact underwater platforms, where the actuator, power electronics, pressure boundary, and control hardware all compete for limited space.

Earlier work has established both the usefulness and the difficulty of buoyancy-driven depth regulation. Tangirala and Dzielski \cite{tangirala2007vbs} studied a variable buoyancy control system for a large AUV and emphasized its nonlinear and delayed behavior. Zhang et al. \cite{zhang2007fish} demonstrated depth control of a biomimetic robotic fish using fin-based actuation. Other robotic-fish and bio-inspired studies considered thrust redirection, fins, artificial swim bladders, and buoyancy-regulation units \cite{islam2021anguilliform,le2011bladder}. Katzschmann et al. \cite{katzschmann2018sofi} presented a soft robotic fish with onboard buoyancy adjustment, and Keow et al. \cite{keow2022fuelcell} developed a reversible-fuel-cell-based buoyancy system with feedback depth control. More recent VBS studies have explored state-feedback hovering control, switching depth control, modular VBS design, and energy-optimal depth regulation \cite{tiwari2020vbs,xia2024vbs,cao2023energy}. Most of these studies, however, stay within PID/PDA, state-feedback, switching, trajectory-planning, energy-optimization, or other model-based control structures rather than online performance optimization.

Table~\ref{tab:literature_gap} places this work relative to those efforts and to the ESC literature. The underwater-control studies show that buoyancy-based depth regulation is practical, but they do not directly address compact cylinder-piston hardware with simultaneous stroke and voltage limits. ESC theory, on the other hand, already contains tools for real-time optimization, unstable systems, and constrained seeking, but usually in more general dynamical-system settings than the device studied here.

