For a compact piston-cylinder buoyancy device, the actuator and the vehicle dynamics cannot be treated independently. Piston displacement changes the displaced volume, which changes net buoyancy and directly affects vertical acceleration. At the same time, the actuator is shaped by electrical, mechanical, and transmission effects. Lead-screw or geared mechanisms can introduce backlash, hysteresis, dead-zone behavior, direction-dependent friction, rate limits, and self-locking effects. These details are difficult to model accurately and can vary with load, pressure, operating direction, wear, and manufacturing tolerances.

The coupling becomes especially important near the hardware limits. A small buoyancy bias can produce persistent upward or downward motion, while stroke and voltage limits reduce the available control authority near the operating boundaries. Model predictive control can handle constraints explicitly \cite{mayne2000constrained}, and our previous work used that property as a baseline for this device class \cite{masood2023constrained}. The drawback is that performance depends strongly on how well the model captures effects such as friction, backlash, dead-zone behavior, and direction-dependent dynamics.

Extremum seeking control (ESC) is attractive in this setting because it optimizes a measured performance signal online without requiring an exact plant model. Classical ESC uses periodic perturbation, demodulation, and filtering to estimate the local performance gradient and update the decision variable toward an improved operating point \cite{krstic2000stability,scheinker2024survey}. That model-light structure is useful for compact underwater hardware whose behavior changes with load, pressure, direction of motion, and device tolerances.

At the same time, ESC is not a plug-in replacement for a conventional controller. Classical interpretations are cleaner when the plant remains stable for fixed parameter values and when the perturbation stays inside a physically meaningful input range. A compact cylinder-piston BCD challenges both assumptions: its depth dynamics may be weakly damped or unstable, while voltage saturation, stroke limits, actuator dead zones, and friction can distort both the nominal command and the injected dither. Prior ESC work has addressed unstable systems \cite{zhang2007unstable,radenkovic2018adaptive} and constrained seeking \cite{dehaan2005state,tan2013constrained,guay2015constrained,labar2019barrier}, but those results do not directly settle the implementation problem for compact underwater buoyancy hardware.

This paper focuses on that practical gap. The target is not ESC theory in the abstract, and not buoyancy control in general, but ESC-based depth regulation for a compact cylinder-piston BCD with coupled depth-actuator dynamics, hard stroke and voltage limits, and actuator nonlinearities that matter in practice.

We approach the problem by treating ESC as an online performance layer shaped by the physical limits of the device rather than as an unconstrained black-box optimizer. The performance signal reflects both depth-regulation quality and control-input utilization, while the adaptation structure is designed to maintain feasible actuator commands near stroke and voltage limits.

The paper makes three contributions. First, it presents an updated compact cylinder-piston buoyancy-control device for robotic-fish and small underwater-platform applications. Second, it develops a physics-based dynamic model that couples piston-actuator motion with vertical depth dynamics, including buoyancy variation, hydrostatic loading, and practical actuator bounds. Third, it develops and evaluates a constraint-aware ESC-based depth-control framework for the resulting unstable hard-bound system.

\section{Design and Dynamic Modeling}
The depth control device uses the buoyancy control principle, which relies on adjusting the volume of the device to control its buoyancy. The device uses a piston-cylinder assembly which is actuated by a linear actuator to change the effective volume of the device, or in other words, the linear actuator pushes the water out of the cylinder to increase the volume of the device, and pulls the water into the cylinder to decrease the volume of the device. The system architecture is shown in Fig. \ref{fig:system_architecture}.

