\subsection{Dynamic Modeling}
The vertical motion of the buoyancy control device is modeled using Newton's second law along the depth axis. Let $z$ denote depth relative to the neutral reference level, with $z=0$ at the equilibrium position used in the free-body diagram (Fig. \ref{fig:system_architecture}). The device mass $m$ is constant, while buoyancy varies due to piston-induced volume change.

The total displaced volume is separated into a fixed part $V_s$ and a controllable part $V_d$, so the buoyant force is $F_b = -\rho_w(V_s+V_d)g$, where $\rho_w$ is water density and $g$ is gravitational acceleration. Neutral buoyancy at $z=0$ requires $m/V_s = \rho_w$. Applying Newton's second law with linear hydrodynamic drag $c_d\dot{z}$ and substituting the neutral-buoyancy condition yields
\[
    \ddot{z} + \frac{c_d}{m}\dot{z} = \frac{g}{V_s}V_d.
\]

The controllable volume is related to piston displacement $x$ and piston radius $r_p$ by $V_d = -\pi r_p^2 x$, which gives the depth dynamics in input-output form:
\begin{equation}
    \ddot{z} + \frac{c_d}{m}\dot{z} = -\frac{g\pi r_p^2}{V_s}x.
    \label{eqn:depth_dynamics}
\end{equation}

Therefore, the buoyancy control device can be represented as a second-order system with piston position $x$ as the control input and depth $z$ as the output.

\subsection{Dynamics of the Piston-Cylinder Assembly}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig/piston-cylinder.png}
\caption{Cross-sectional schematic of the piston-cylinder assembly.}
\label{fig:piston_cylinder}
\end{figure}

The piston-cylinder assembly is driven by a DC linear actuator. Using the standard armature equation, the electrical dynamics of the actuator are written as
\begin{equation}
    R_a i_a + L_a \frac{d i_a}{dt} + V_b = u,
    \label{eqn:armature_full}
\end{equation}
where $i_a$ is the armature current, $R_a$ is the armature resistance, $L_a$ is the armature inductance, $V_b$ is the back-emf voltage, and $u$ is the applied input voltage. For the present actuator, the armature inductance is small relative to the dominant mechanical time scale, so $L_a\approx 0$, and the back-emf is proportional to piston velocity, $V_b = K_b'\dot{x}$. Substituting both simplifications into \eqref{eqn:armature_full} gives
\begin{equation}
    R_a i_a + K_b'\dot{x} = u,
    \label{eqn:armature_reduced}
\end{equation}
from which the armature current is immediately obtained as
\begin{equation}
    i_a = \frac{u - K_b'\dot{x}}{R_a}.
    \label{eqn:armature_current}
\end{equation}

For the basic mechanical model of the actuator, the piston-force balance is written as
\[
    m_l \ddot{x} = -f_l\dot{x} + K_f i_a,
\]
where $m_l$ is the equivalent moving mass, $f_l$ is the equivalent viscous damping coefficient, and $K_f$ is the actuator force constant. Substituting \eqref{eqn:armature_current} and rearranging,
\[
    R_a m_l\ddot{x} = -\left(R_a f_l + K_f K_b'\right)\dot{x} + K_f u.
\]
Therefore, the reduced actuator model becomes
\begin{equation}
    \ddot{x} = -\frac{R_a f_l + K_f K_b'}{m_l R_a}\dot{x} + \frac{K_f}{m_l R_a}u.
    \label{eqn:actuator_reduced_raw}
\end{equation}
Introducing the time constant and static gain
\begin{equation}
    \tau = \frac{m_l R_a}{R_a f_l + K_f K_b'},
    \qquad
    K_l = \frac{K_f}{R_a f_l + K_f K_b'},
    \label{eqn:actuator_tau_gain}
\end{equation}
allows \eqref{eqn:actuator_reduced_raw} to be expressed in the compact form $\tau\ddot{x} + \dot{x} = K_l u$, or equivalently,
\begin{equation}
    \ddot{x} = -\frac{1}{\tau}\dot{x} + \frac{K_l}{\tau}u.
    \label{eqn:actuator_reduced}
\end{equation}

To couple the actuator with the buoyancy dynamics, the hydrostatic loading and enclosed-pressure restoring force must also be included. With piston area $A_p = \pi r_p^2$, the extended mechanical model is
\[
    m_l\ddot{x} = -f_l\dot{x} + K_f i_a + A_p\rho_w g z - \frac{A_p^2P_s}{V_{se}}x,
\]
where $P_s$ is the equilibrium internal pressure and $V_{se}$ is the equilibrium enclosed volume. Substituting \eqref{eqn:armature_current} and applying the definitions in \eqref{eqn:actuator_tau_gain} yields $\tau\ddot{x} + \dot{x} = K_l u + (\tau/m_l)A_p\rho_w g z - (\tau/m_l)(A_p^2P_s/V_{se})x$, or, equivalently,
\begin{equation}
    \ddot{x} = -\frac{1}{\tau}\dot{x} + \frac{K_l}{\tau}u - \frac{A_p\rho_w g}{m_l}z - \frac{A_p^2P_s}{m_lV_{se}}x.
    \label{eqn:actuator_extended}
\end{equation}

Combining \eqref{eqn:actuator_extended} with the depth model \eqref{eqn:depth_dynamics} gives a coupled fourth-order system with states $[z,\dot{z},x,\dot{x}]^T$, where depth is driven by piston motion and the piston dynamics are influenced by actuator input, hydrostatic loading, and internal pressure feedback. The state-space representation is
\begin{equation}
    \label{eqn:state_space}
    \begin{bmatrix}\dot{z} \\ \ddot{z} \\ \dot{x} \\ \ddot{x}\end{bmatrix} =
    \begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & -\frac{c_d}{m} & -\frac{g\pi r_p^2}{V_s} & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\
0 & -\frac{A_p\rho_w g}{m_l} & -\frac{A_p^2P_s}{m_lV_{se}} & -\frac{1}{\tau}\end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}z \\ \dot{z} \\ x \\ \dot{x}\end{bmatrix} +
    \begin{bmatrix}0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ \frac{K_l}{\tau}\end{bmatrix}u.
\end{equation}

Table~\ref{tab:model_parameters} summarizes the geometric, hydraulic, and actuator parameters used in the model. The device mass is taken as $m=m_d=\rho_w V_s$ from the neutral-buoyancy condition, the drag coefficient is denoted by $c_d$, and the equilibrium chamber pressure is approximated as $P_s=P_a$.

