\subsection{Physical Modeling Considerations and Finalized Model}

The complete coupled model in \eqref{eqn:state_space} assumes that the external hydrostatic pressure and internal chamber pressure are directly transmitted to the actuator dynamics. This represents the fully coupled pressure-loaded case. However, the actual hardware contains two important physical effects that must be considered before finalizing the model: neutral-buoyancy offset and actuator self-locking.

Let the piston area be
\[
    A_p=\pi r_p^2,
\]
and define the state vector as
\[
    \boldsymbol{\xi}=\begin{bmatrix} z & \dot{z} & x & \dot{x} \end{bmatrix}^T,
\]

where \(z\) is the depth displacement and \(x\) is the piston displacement.

\subsubsection{Buoyancy Offset and Affine Term}

The nominal model assumes that the device is neutrally buoyant when the piston displacement is zero. In practice, the physical piston zero may not coincide with the neutral-buoyancy point. The neutral point can shift due to payload variation, trapped air, manufacturing tolerance, water absorption, or leakage.

Let \(x_0\) denote the piston offset required for neutral buoyancy. Then the effective piston displacement contributing to buoyancy is
\[
    x_{\mathrm{eff}}=x-x_0.
\]

Using this definition in \eqref{eqn:depth_dynamics}, the depth dynamics become
\[
    \ddot{z}=-\frac{c_d}{m}\dot{z}-\frac{gA_p}{V_s}(x-x_0).
\]

Expanding gives
\[
    \ddot{z}=-\frac{c_d}{m}\dot{z}-\frac{gA_p}{V_s}x+\frac{gA_p}{V_s}x_0.
\]

The last term is a constant offset. Therefore, the system becomes affine rather than purely linear when \(x_0\neq 0\). If \(x_0\) is constant during an experiment, the model is affine time-invariant. If \(x_0\) changes slowly with time, the model can be treated as an LTI plant with a slowly varying disturbance.

\subsubsection{Self-Locking and Piecewise Pressure Coupling}

The linear actuator is based on a lead-screw mechanism and therefore has a finite backdrive threshold. This means that pressure loads below the backdrive force do not move the actuator when the input voltage is zero. Therefore, the pressure terms in the actuator dynamics should not always be active.

The pressure-induced load on the actuator can be written as
\begin{equation}
    F_p(z,x)=-A_p\rho_wgz-\frac{A_p^2P_s}{V_{se}}x.
    \label{eqn:pressure_load}
\end{equation}

Let \(F_{\mathrm{bd}}\) denote the actuator backdrive force. Then the pressure coupling is active only when
\[
    |F_p(z,x)|>F_{\mathrm{bd}}.
\]

This produces a piecewise model. Define the switching variable
\begin{equation}
    \sigma(z,x)=
    \begin{cases}
        0, & |F_p(z,x)|\leq F_{\mathrm{bd}},\\
        1, & |F_p(z,x)|>F_{\mathrm{bd}}.
    \end{cases}
    \label{eqn:switching_sigma}
\end{equation}

When \(\sigma=0\), the actuator is self-locked against the pressure load and the actuator dynamics are decoupled from the depth pressure terms. When \(\sigma=1\), the pressure load exceeds the self-locking threshold and the full coupled dynamics are active.

The finalized state-space model is therefore
\begin{equation}
    \dot{\boldsymbol{\xi}}=A_{\sigma}\boldsymbol{\xi}+Bu+d_0,
    \label{eqn:finalized_hybrid_model}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
    A_{\sigma}=\begin{bmatrix}
        0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\
        0 & -\dfrac{c_d}{m} & -\dfrac{gA_p}{V_s} & 0 \\
        0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\
        -\sigma\dfrac{A_p\rho_wg}{m_l} & 0 & -\sigma\dfrac{A_p^2P_s}{m_lV_{se}} & -\dfrac{1}{\tau}
    \end{bmatrix},
    \label{eqn:Asigma}
\end{equation}
\[
    B=\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & \dfrac{K_l}{\tau}\end{bmatrix}^T,
\]
and
\[
    d_0=\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\dfrac{gA_p}{V_s}x_0 & 0 & 0\end{bmatrix}^T.
\]

For shallow-water operation, where the pressure-induced load is below the actuator backdrive threshold, \(\sigma=0\), and \eqref{eqn:Asigma} reduces to the decoupled form
\begin{equation}
    A_{0}=\begin{bmatrix}
        0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\
        0 & -\dfrac{c_d}{m} & -\dfrac{gA_p}{V_s} & 0 \\
        0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\
        0 & 0 & 0 & -\dfrac{1}{\tau}
    \end{bmatrix}.
    \label{eqn:A0_decoupled}
\end{equation}

For deeper operation, where the pressure-induced load exceeds the backdrive threshold, \(\sigma=1\), and \eqref{eqn:Asigma} becomes the complete coupled pressure-loaded model
\[
    A_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}
        0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\
        0 & -\dfrac{c_d}{m} & -\dfrac{gA_p}{V_s} & 0 \\
        0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\
        -\dfrac{A_p\rho_wg}{m_l} & 0 & -\dfrac{A_p^2P_s}{m_lV_{se}} & -\dfrac{1}{\tau}
    \end{bmatrix}.
\]

Thus, the final model is a piecewise-affine (hybrid) state-space model in \eqref{eqn:finalized_hybrid_model}. It is affine because of the constant offset term $d_0$, and piecewise nonlinear because the actuator pressure coupling switches according to \eqref{eqn:switching_sigma}.

\subsection{System Analysis}
Both representative linear modes, the coupled model in \eqref{eqn:state_space} and the decoupled mode in \eqref{eqn:A0_decoupled}, are unstable, as the eigenvalues of their system matrices have positive real parts. The system is also non-minimum phase, as the zero dynamics are unstable. The system has hard input constraints, since the piston travel and actuator voltage are both bounded. These constraints must be incorporated in controller design, because violating them can lead to instability and hardware risk.

