Before discussing the design of the controller for the buoyancy control device, the authors want to focus on following facts:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The system does not have a static map for optimization, as the system is dynamic and the output depends on the history of the input, neutral buoyancy bias, and other factors.
    \item The system does not have a single extremum point, as the system can have multiple local minima and maxima depending on the operating conditions and the input trajectory.
    \item The hard bounds on the input and the state variables can lead to instability and misguide the optimization process. All these must be handled correctly to make use of the extremum seeking control for the system.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Control Technique 1: Direct-Input ESC Baseline}
The first control technique considered in this work is the direct application of ESC to the complete coupled model in \eqref{eqn:state_space}. The actuator voltage is treated as the optimization variable, with input
\[
u(t)=\hat{u}(t)+b\sin(\omega t),
\]
where $\hat{u}(t)$ is the slowly varying estimate and $b\sin(\omega t)$ is the dither signal. The objective is selected from depth-tracking error,
\[
J(t)=-(z(t)-r(t))^2,
\]
and the demodulated/filtered ESC signal updates $\hat{u}(t)$ to improve $J$.

This structure is used as a baseline because ESC acts directly on the physical input. However, for the coupled BCD model, voltage does not directly determine depth; it first drives piston dynamics, and depth then evolves through the full four-state dynamics. Using the state vector from \eqref{eqn:state_space},
\[
\mathbf{x}=\begin{bmatrix}z & \dot{z} & x & \dot{x}\end{bmatrix}^T,
\]
the dynamics can be written in affine form as
\[
\dot{\mathbf{x}}=A\mathbf{x}+Bu+f.
\]
Hence the measured objective is trajectory dependent,
\[
J=J(\mathbf{x}(t),u(t),r(t)),
\]
rather than a purely static map of $\hat{u}$. Consequently, direct-voltage ESC on the coupled model has an inherent limitation: the adaptation law modifies the input estimate, but it does not alter the unstable internal dynamics represented by $A$. The architecture in Fig.~\ref{fig:control_technique_1} is therefore best viewed as a conceptual baseline, which motivates introducing additional internal stabilization in the next technique.

\subsection{Control Technique 2: ESC with Inner-Loop Actuator Stabilization}
As a refinement of Technique~1, an inner PID loop is introduced to regulate piston position $x$ to a command generated by the outer ESC loop (Fig.~\ref{fig:control_technique_2}). This modification improves actuator-side behavior, but it does not close the depth loop. Under the decoupled mode in \eqref{eqn:A0_decoupled}, once the inner loop is sufficiently fast ($x\approx x_{\rm des}$), the depth channel is governed by
\[
\ddot{z}+\frac{c_d}{m}\dot{z}=-\frac{g\pi r_p^2}{V_s}x_{\rm des}.
\]
For constant $x_{\rm des}$, this dynamics does not define a finite steady depth; instead, it produces a nonzero asymptotic depth rate, so depth keeps drifting in uniform water. Consequently, the ESC objective remains trajectory dependent and cannot be represented by a well-defined static map in the optimization variable. In other words, adding an inner actuator loop alone is not sufficient; the depth state itself must be stabilized before ESC can provide meaningful optimization.

