\subsection{Control Technique 3: Proposed ESC-Tuned PD Depth Controller}
The third technique uses ESC to tune the gains of a proportional-derivative (PD) depth controller online. The inner control law is a PD controller acting directly on depth:
\begin{equation}
    u = K_p\bigl(r - z\bigr) - K_d\,\dot{z},
    \label{eqn:pd_law}
\end{equation}
where $r$ is the depth reference, $z$ is the measured depth, $\dot{z}$ is the depth rate, $K_p > 0$ is the proportional gain, and $K_d > 0$ is the derivative gain. The proportional term drives depth toward the reference, while the derivative term damps oscillations by penalising depth velocity. There is no integral term, so the inner loop is a pure PD controller.

The ESC layer sits on top and tunes $K_p$ and $K_d$ online rather than fixing them a priori. A scalar cost function is defined as
\begin{equation}
    J = \dot{x}^{\,2},
    \label{eqn:cost_esc3}
\end{equation}
which penalizes actuator motion and is minimized by the ESC adaptation.  An alternative cost that explicitly promotes stroke-limit avoidance replaces the velocity penalty with a deadzone penalty on piston position,
\begin{equation}
    J_2 = w_z\,\bigl[\max\!\bigl(0,\,|z_a - z_{a,\mathrm{eq}}| - \delta_z\bigr)\bigr]^{2},
    \label{eqn:cost_esc3_deadzone}
\end{equation}
where $z_a$ is the piston position, $z_{a,\mathrm{eq}}$ is the nominal piston equilibrium, $\delta_z$ is the deadzone half-width, and $w_z > 0$ is a scalar weight.  When the piston remains within the deadzone $|z_a - z_{a,\mathrm{eq}}| \leq \delta_z$, the cost is identically zero, so ESC dithering produces no gradient estimate and the gains are held constant.  As soon as the piston strays outside this band---approaching the hard stroke limits---the cost rises quadratically and ESC pushes the gains in a direction that pulls the piston back toward center stroke.  Each gain is independently perturbed by a distinct sinusoidal dither signal ($a_p\sin\omega_p t$ for $K_p$ and $a_d\sin\omega_d t$ for $K_d$, with $\omega_p \neq \omega_d$ to avoid cross-channel interference). The standard ESC gradient-estimation chain (HPF $\to$ demodulation $\to$ LPF $\to$ integration) is applied separately to each channel, yielding online updates $\hat{K}_p(t)$ and $\hat{K}_d(t)$ that converge to a locally improving gain pair without requiring any plant model. The full architecture, an ESC-tuned PD depth controller applied to the fourth-order coupled BCD plant, is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:control_technique_3}.

