\section{Model Validation}
The depth control device, named DiveSync, is designed for laboratory testing while remaining scalable to real underwater applications.
A cylindrical enclosure achieves IP67-standard liquid-immersion safety using O-ring vacuum face sealing with a 10\% compression ratio.
Small buckets are designed into the bottom portion to hold dead weights, allowing the device mass and weight balance to be adjusted so that the device is neutrally buoyant with no control input; the bucket geometry also permits the center of mass to be tuned by varying the mass distribution among the buckets.
The enclosure is fabricated using a FormLabs Form~3 Stereolithography (SLA)~\cite{form3_printer} printer with Clear Resin material.
The CAD design of the depth control device is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:device_cad}, and the fabricated device is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:device_photo}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\columnwidth}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig/DiveSyn_IsoView.JPG}
    \caption{Isometric CAD view of DiveSync.}
\end{subfigure}\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\columnwidth}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig/DiveSyn_SectionActuator.JPG}
    \caption{Section CAD view showing actuator integration.}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{DiveSync CAD views used for device design documentation.}
\label{fig:device_cad}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{fig/bcd_hardware.png}
\caption{Fabricated DiveSync device used in experiments.}
\label{fig:device_photo}
\end{figure}



A waterproof cylinder-and-piston mechanism is designed and fabricated alongside the enclosure.
The piston is actuated by an Actuonix Motion Devices L12 miniature linear actuator with integrated translational-potentiometer feedback~\cite{actuonix_l12}.
The power source, application-level microcontroller, and motor-driving circuitry are all self-contained within the device to avoid disturbances from external harness routing.
An ESP32-C3 microcontroller from Espressif Systems~\cite{esp32_c3} drives the linear actuator through a Texas Instruments motor-driver IC, and an LPS33HW water-pressure sensor from STMicroelectronics provides depth feedback.
Communication between the device and the host computer is established over a USB serial COM port, with a Python-based host application handling real-time data logging, command generation, and visualization.

\subsection{Open-Loop Validation of Dynamic Model}
Because the actuator has built-in self-locking up to 50\,N of holding force, and the hydrostatic load remains below that threshold to a depth of 1\,m, the uncoupled model represents the system in this operating range. To validate the full coupled model, an open-loop experiment was conducted by applying a square-wave voltage input $u(t)$ to the linear actuator and simultaneously recording the actuator position $z_a(t)$ and the device depth $z_d(t)$. The measured responses are compared against the model-simulated outputs in Fig.~\ref{fig:open_loop_validation}. The actuator response shows close agreement with a root-mean-square error (RMSE) of 1.96\,mm, confirming the identified time constant and gain. The depth response reproduces the qualitative oscillatory behavior with an RMSE of 23.07\,mm; the residual error is attributed to unmodeled hydrodynamic drag variation and wall effects in the laboratory tank.



\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig/open_loop_validation.pdf}
\caption{Open-loop model validation: $u(t)$, $z_a(t)$, and $z_d(t)$; measurements (solid), model (dashed).}
\label{fig:open_loop_validation}
\end{figure}

Applying a control technique, for example even PID, would capture the neutral buoyancy point because the controller adjusts the input to maintain the depth at the reference. However, open-loop validation requires a precise estimate of the neutral-buoyancy equilibrium point. In practice, that point is difficult to determine directly and is identified by repeating the simulation with different equilibrium values until the best fit is found. If the equilibrium point is not correctly identified, the simulated depth response will show a drift, which is not observed in the experimental data. This suggests that the equilibrium point is correctly identified and that the model captures the essential dynamics of the system.

\section{Results and Discussion}
After model validation, the control workflow is first verified on the actuator subsystem and then extended to the full coupled depth system. Techniques~2 and~3 rely on reliable actuator-side command tracking before depth-loop behavior can be evaluated.

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
The actuator-loop experiments are executed with an ESP32-based embedded node and a Python host interface running on the PC. The ESP32 streams telemetry over USB serial, while the host computes the actuator control action and transmits motor commands back to the embedded side. The host interface also provides live visualization and synchronized logging of actuator position, command activity, and depth/pressure telemetry.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.82\columnwidth]{fig/ActuatorControlGUI.png}
\caption{Host-side interface used for actuator-loop experiments, parameter tuning, telemetry monitoring, and safety control.}
\label{fig:actuator_control_gui}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig/ActuatorControl_Result.pdf}
\caption{Actuator-loop experiment: actuator tracking (top), sent/echoed command activity (middle), and measured depth response (bottom).}
\label{fig:actuator_control_result}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{fig/DepthControlSim_ESCPD_O4_V1_sine_then_square.pdf}
\caption{ESC-tuned PD depth-control simulation with sine-to-square reference switching, showing depth tracking, gain adaptation, control effort, piston motion, and ESC cost/gradient signals.}
\label{fig:escpd_sine_square_result}
\end{figure*}

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{fig/DepthControlSim_ESCPD_O4_V1_20260508_140237.pdf}
\caption{ESC-tuned PD simulation under the deadzone piston-position penalty \eqref{eqn:cost_esc3_deadzone} ($w_z=2.0$, $\delta_z=15$\,mm) with sine-to-square reference switching.  Compared with the velocity-penalty case (Fig.~\ref{fig:escpd_sine_square_result}), the piston remains more strictly within its stroke limits, demonstrating that the choice of ESC cost directly governs constraint-respecting behavior.}
\label{fig:escpd_deadzone_result}
\end{figure*}


The actuator position is measured in ADC counts and converted to millimeters using
\[
    x_{a,\mathrm{mm}}=\frac{50}{4096}\,x_{a,\mathrm{raw}},
\]
and the motor command is mapped to equivalent drive voltage as
\[
    u_{\mathrm{V}}=\frac{12}{255}\,u_{\mathrm{cmd}}.
\]

\subsection{Actuator Control}
The actuator experiment uses a single-loop position controller (not the cascaded depth controller). In closed-loop mode, the host computes error in actuator-count coordinates as
\[
    e_k=x_{a,k}-x_{a,\mathrm{ref},k},
\]
with deadband and integral limiting applied for robustness. A filtered-derivative PID form is used:
\[
    u_k^{\ast}=K_p e_k+K_i I_k+K_d d_k,
\]
where
\[
    d_k=\alpha\frac{e_k-e_{k-1}}{\Delta t_k}+(1-\alpha)d_{k-1}.
\]
The unsaturated control is then scaled to the command interface and clipped to hardware limits:
\begin{equation}
    u_{\mathrm{cmd},k}=\mathrm{sat}_{[-255,255]}\!\left(\frac{255}{4000}u_k^{\ast}\right).
    \label{eqn:act_cmd_saturation}
\end{equation}

To reduce jitter from raw ADC measurements, two causal conditioning stages are available. First, a sliding-window median filter produces $x_{a,\mathrm{med},k}$. Second, an $\alpha$-$\beta$ estimator updates actuator position/velocity states by
\begin{equation}
    \hat{x}_{k|k-1}=\hat{x}_{k-1}+\Delta t_k\hat{v}_{k-1}, \qquad
    r_k=x_{a,\mathrm{med},k}-\hat{x}_{k|k-1},
    \label{eqn:ab_predict_residual}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \hat{x}_k=\hat{x}_{k|k-1}+\alpha_{ab}r_k, \qquad
    \hat{v}_k=\lambda_v\hat{v}_{k-1}+\frac{\beta_{ab}}{\Delta t_k}r_k.
    \label{eqn:ab_update}
\end{equation}
The controller can use raw, median-filtered, or estimated actuator position as its feedback input source.

Figure~\ref{fig:actuator_control_result} shows a representative actuator-loop experiment. The top panel indicates accurate command tracking across multiple setpoint changes, with the median signal closely following the target and with reduced oscillatory noise compared with raw telemetry. The middle panel shows that sent and echoed commands are consistent, while brief transients occur at direction reversals and large setpoint steps. The bottom panel reports depth telemetry during the same run; depth evolves with actuator motion as expected from buoyancy modulation, while the present experiment remains focused on actuator-loop validation rather than closed-loop depth regulation.

These actuator-loop results also expose implementation effects omitted from the model, including motor dead zone, command saturation, and feedback fluctuations. In the experiment, those effects are handled explicitly so that the actuator remains suitable for the subsequent ESC studies.
The host-side actuator loop therefore provides stable, repeatable position tracking and sufficiently smooth command behavior for subsequent cascaded experiments. In particular, the measurement-conditioning pipeline in \eqref{eqn:ab_predict_residual}--\eqref{eqn:ab_update} reduces control jitter without removing the dynamic response needed for outer-loop tuning.

\subsection{ESC-tuned PD Controller}
For the ESC-tuned PD study, the reference is intentionally switched from a sinusoid to a square wave in a single run to test both smooth and abrupt operating regimes under the same adaptation law.  Two cost formulations are evaluated in simulation.  In the first, the cost \eqref{eqn:cost_esc3} is implemented as the squared piston velocity, so ESC seeks gains that reduce overall actuator motion while maintaining acceptable depth tracking.  In the second, the deadzone penalty \eqref{eqn:cost_esc3_deadzone} replaces the velocity cost; here ESC specifically penalizes piston-position excursions beyond a safe band around the neutral stroke, directly discouraging operation near the hard stroke limits.

\subsubsection{Velocity-penalty cost}
Figure~\ref{fig:escpd_sine_square_result} shows the closed-loop behavior under cost \eqref{eqn:cost_esc3}. During the sinusoidal segment, depth tracking remains smooth with moderate gain adaptation. After the reference switches to square-wave motion, ESC increases and redistributes the effective PD gains to handle repeated transient events while attempting to keep actuator-motion cost low. The command plot confirms that voltage remains within the imposed $\pm 12$\,V bounds, and the piston trajectory remains within the prescribed stroke limits. The gradient/cost panel shows distinct adaptation bursts at transition instants, which is consistent with the nonsmooth reference and the motion-penalty objective.  However, because the cost does not directly penalize proximity to the stroke bounds, the piston occasionally approaches the hard limits at the onset of aggressive square-wave transients.

\subsubsection{Deadzone piston-position penalty}
Figure~\ref{fig:escpd_deadzone_result} shows the closed-loop behavior under the deadzone penalty \eqref{eqn:cost_esc3_deadzone} with parameters $w_z = 2.0$, $\delta_z = 15$\,mm, and $z_{a,\mathrm{eq}}$ set to the neutral stroke midpoint.  The key behavioral difference relative to the velocity-penalty case is that the piston now remains more tightly confined within the safe band.  Whenever a large-amplitude square-wave step drives the piston toward a hard limit, the deadzone cost activates and ESC adapts the gains to reduce the excursion, trading some transient depth-tracking aggressiveness for improved stroke-limit clearance.  Voltage commands still respect the $\pm 12$\,V input bound, and depth tracking quality is comparable to the velocity-penalty case, confirming that the deadzone penalty achieves better bound satisfaction without degrading the outer-loop depth-regulation objective.

Comparing the two results, the deadzone penalty provides a more direct mechanism for constraint enforcement: the ESC gradient is nonzero only when the piston is outside the safe band, so adaptation effort is targeted precisely at bound-violation events.  The velocity penalty, by contrast, reduces overall actuator activity but does not distinguish between beneficial motion (depth correction) and undesirable motion (bound encroachment), resulting in occasional excursions near the limits.  These results are a preliminary validation of the proposed Technique~3 under mixed reference profiles and demonstrate that the choice of ESC objective directly shapes the constraint-respecting behavior of the closed-loop system.  

\subsection{Experimental Validation of ESC-tuned PD Controller}

Physical experiments introduce disturbances and nonlinearities that are absent from the simulation model. Two effects are particularly significant in this device. First, the linear actuator exhibits a pronounced dead zone: control voltages below a certain threshold produce no piston motion, because the motor torque is insufficient to overcome static friction and the transmission backlash. This nonlinearity means that small ESC corrections may be silently absorbed at the actuator, and the piston moves only once the command exceeds the threshold. Second, the sensor and tether cable, though neutrally buoyant by construction, is not perfectly limp. Cable tension and stiffness vary with the relative position of the device in the tank, imposing a slowly varying external force disturbance that cannot be modelled or anticipated. Together, these effects reduce the effective gain seen by the outer depth loop and introduce low-frequency drift that the ESC must compensate online.

Given these challenges, several candidate cost formulations were evaluated over repeated trials. The composite cost
\begin{equation}
    J = \bigl(r - \hat{z}\bigr)^{2} + w_{z}\,\bigl[\max\!\bigl(0,\,|z_{a} - z_{a,\mathrm{eq}}| - \delta_{z}\bigr)\bigr]^{2}
    \label{eqn:cost_exp}
\end{equation}
was found to provide the most reliable and safe closed-loop behavior. The first term is the primary depth-tracking objective: it drives the ESC gradient toward gains that reduce the squared depth error $(r - \hat{z})^2$. The second term is the deadzone piston-position penalty from \eqref{eqn:cost_esc3_deadzone}: it activates only when the piston strays beyond $\delta_z$ from the neutral stroke $z_{a,\mathrm{eq}}$, at which point it penalizes further excursion and steers the ESC adaptation away from gains that push the piston toward its hard limits. When the piston remains within the safe band the penalty is identically zero, so ESC focuses exclusively on depth-tracking improvement. This two-term formulation is preferred over a pure velocity penalty because it provides a direct coupling between the depth objective and constraint enforcement: gains are not reduced indiscriminately but only when tracking improvement would come at the cost of stroke-limit proximity. The derivative gain is not adapted independently; instead, the fixed ratio $K_d = T_D K_p$ with $T_D = 10$\,s is maintained throughout, so a single ESC channel tunes $\hat{K}_p$ and $K_d$ tracks proportionally.

The experimental parameters are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:esc_exp_params}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{ESC-tuned PD experimental parameters.}
\label{tab:esc_exp_params}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
\textbf{Parameter} & \textbf{Symbol} & \textbf{Value} \\
\hline
Derivative time constant        & $T_D$             & $10.0$\,s \\
Initial proportional gain       & $K_{p,0}$         & $15.0$ \\
Gain lower bound                & $K_{p,\min}$      & $1.0$ \\
Gain upper bound                & $K_{p,\max}$      & $35.0$ \\
ESC integration gain            & $\gamma$          & $5000.0$ \\
Dither frequency                & $\omega_{\mathrm{ESC}}$ & $\pi$\,rad/s \\
Dither amplitude                & $a$               & $1.0$ \\
Demodulation gain               & $b$               & $2.0$ \\
Demodulation phase offset       & $\phi$            & $90^{\circ}$ \\
Piston deadzone weight          & $w_z$             & $2.0$ \\
Piston deadzone half-width      & $\delta_z$        & $15$\,mm \\
Piston equilibrium              & $z_{a,\mathrm{eq}}$ & $25$\,mm \\
Piston stroke limits            & $[z_{a,\min},\,z_{a,\max}]$ & $[0,\;50]$\,mm \\
Voltage limits                  & $[u_{\min},\,u_{\max}]$ & $[-12,\;+12]$\,V \\
ESC update period               & $\Delta t_{\mathrm{ESC}}$ & $50$\,ms \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Figure~\ref{fig:escpd_exp_result} presents the experimental results under a square-wave depth reference alternating between $z = 200$\,mm and $z = 500$\,mm. The four panels show depth tracking ($r$, raw depth $z$, and Kalman-estimated depth $\hat{z}$), gain evolution ($K_p$ dithered, its DC estimate $\hat{K}_p$, and $K_d = T_D \hat{K}_p$), control voltage $u$, and piston position $z_a$, respectively.

The first reference period ($t \approx 0$--$50$\,s) is operated under a sinusoidal reference with the initial gain $K_{p,0} = 15$. This gain is intentionally chosen above the steady-state optimal to stress the adaptation law from the start. The depth response shows substantial oscillatory overshoot: the depth trajectory undershoots and overshoots the reference by several tens of millimetres, and the piston traverses most of its stroke range. The ESC cost \eqref{eqn:cost_exp} accumulates a persistent nonzero gradient because the depth error and occasional piston excursions beyond $\delta_z$ both contribute, and the adaptation law begins to reduce $\hat{K}_p$.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.88\textwidth]{fig/DepthControlExp_ESCPD_O420260513_164014_overview.pdf}
\caption{ESC-tuned PD depth-control experiment showing depth tracking, gain adaptation, control voltage, and piston displacement. The controller maintains bounded voltage and piston motion while adapting the PD gains online.}
\label{fig:escpd_exp_result}
\end{figure*}


At $t \approx 50$\,s the reference switches to a square wave. By this point the ESC has driven $\hat{K}_p$ noticeably below $K_{p,0}$. The step responses in subsequent cycles show progressively more damped behavior: overshoot is reduced, and the depth settles close to the reference within each half-period. From approximately $t = 100$\,s onward, the gain stabilizes at a lower value and the depth tracking is largely consistent from cycle to cycle, demonstrating that the ESC has found a locally improved operating point. The overall RMSE is $119.3$\,mm, dominated by the initial high-gain transients; the steady-state error after convergence is $16.5$\,mm.

The control voltage (third panel) stays strictly within the $\pm 12$\,V bounds throughout the run. Brief saturation-like excursions near the bound occur at reference steps when the depth error is large, but the voltage returns within bounds as the error reduces. The actuator dead zone is visible as short intervals where $u$ is near zero but the piston does not move, most apparent in the transition plateaus between steps. The piston position (fourth panel) remains within $[0,\;50]$\,mm at all times. The deadzone penalty term in \eqref{eqn:cost_exp} prevents the piston from sustaining operation near the hard limits: whenever the piston moves beyond $z_{a,\mathrm{eq}} \pm \delta_z = 25 \pm 15$\,mm, the cost activates and the ESC immediately adjusts $\hat{K}_p$ to pull the piston back toward center stroke. The depth error term simultaneously ensures that this constraint enforcement does not cause the gain to collapse to the minimum bound, so the device retains tracking authority throughout.


The experimental results confirm the main properties of the proposed controller. Depth tracking is achieved without a plant model, relying entirely on the measured cost signal to tune $\hat{K}_p$ online. Hard actuator constraints are respected: the voltage stays within $\pm 12$\,V and the piston stays within $[0,\;50]$\,mm despite the dead zone, cable disturbances, and step reference changes. The composite cost \eqref{eqn:cost_exp} is effective because its two terms are complementary: the depth-error term drives adaptation toward tracking improvement, while the deadzone penalty activates selectively to prevent constraint violation without unnecessarily suppressing gain. Compared with the simulation results, the experimental transients are longer and the residual steady-state error is larger, consistent with the unmodeled dead zone and cable-tension disturbance. Nevertheless, the ESC framework accommodates these effects through online adaptation and achieves stable, bounded depth regulation in hardware.



This paper presented an extremum-seeking depth-control framework for a hard-bound unstable buoyancy control device intended for compact underwater robotic platforms. The study combined: (i) an updated hardware implementation of a piston-cylinder buoyancy module with linear actuation, (ii) a physics-based fourth-order dynamic model that captures depth-actuator coupling and practical loading effects, and (iii) a cascaded ESC-tuned PD controller that adapts gains online without requiring an exact plant model.

The actuator-loop experiments verified that the host-side position controller achieves stable and repeatable tracking despite practical nonlinearities such as deadband, saturation, and measurement jitter. This inner-loop reliability enabled closed-loop ESC studies with mixed sine-to-square depth references. For the ESC layer, two objective designs were compared. The velocity-penalty objective reduced overall actuator activity and maintained bounded operation, but could still allow transient motion near stroke limits under aggressive reference changes. The deadzone piston-position penalty provided a more direct constraint-aware objective: adaptation was activated primarily when the piston approached unsafe regions, resulting in tighter bound satisfaction while preserving comparable depth-tracking performance and maintaining voltage limits.

Overall, the results demonstrate that ESC can be effectively applied to this unstable, input-constrained buoyancy system, and that objective-function design is a key lever for balancing tracking performance and safety-margin preservation. In particular, penalties tied to bound proximity are more effective than generic motion penalties when strict constraint respect is the primary requirement.

Future work will focus on extended experimental validation in water, robustness analysis under parametric uncertainty and disturbances, and systematic multi-objective ESC formulations that jointly weight depth error, control effort, and constraint margin. Additional directions include adaptive deadzone shaping, anti-windup/constraint-handling enhancements, and real-time implementation on embedded hardware for fully autonomous robotic-fish operation.

